  {
   "id": 535217,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Potassium channel blockers, such as amiodarone and procainamide, which are used to treat abnormal electrical activity in the heart, impede the movement of K+through voltage-gated K+channels. Which part of the action potential would potassium channels affect, and why?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1047372,
     "content_html": "Depolarization after peak action potential would be affected because that is the point when K+ begins to leave the cell."
    },
    {
     "id": 1047373,
     "content_html": "Repolarization after peak action potential would be affected because that is the point when K+ begins to leave the cell."
    },
    {
     "id": 1047374,
     "content_html": "Repolarization after peak action potential would be affected because that is the point when K+ begins to enter the cell."
    },
    {
     "id": 1047375,
     "content_html": "Polarization after peak action potential would be affected because that is the point when K+ begins to enter the cell."
    }
   ],
